The coefficients in @tbl-spatial-sar do not represent the marginal effects of the predictors, thus we refer to @tbl-sar-effects for this.
Our focus is the interaction specification because of the conditional interpretation it provides^[In a SAR model the estimated coefficient vector $\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}$ does not directly recover marginal effects due to the spatial multiplier $(I - \rho W)^{-1}$. The total parametric impacts reported in @tbl-sar-effects -- derived following @lesage2009 -- decompose each covariate's total effect into a direct component (own-region response, averaging the diagonal of the partial derivative matrix $\partial \mathbf{y}/\partial \mathbf{x}_k$) and an indirect component (average spillover to all other regions, averaging the off-diagonal entries). It is these decomposed total impacts, not the raw coefficient vector, that recover the correct marginal effects and are used throughout].


Before interpreting these effects, a note on what the network measures mean here.
In Models 1 and 2, betweenness and eigenvector describe the position of the target technology; in Model 3 they describe the average composition of a region's portfolio.
Are the results then contradictory, since enabling technologies are difficult entry targets (H2b) but valuable portfolio holdings (H5c)?
The two results describe different objects, a candidate technology's position versus a region's existing endowment, and are measured at different levels of analysis.
Their coefficients are therefore not directly comparable: a technology that is difficult to enter can still be valuable to hold.

The marginal effect of average portfolio betweenness centrality on regional potential is entirely conditioned by the level of average industrial coherence in the regional portfolio.
From the interaction SAR specification (@tbl-sar-effects), the conditional total marginal effect of average betweenness(when average coherence = 0) is $0.0140$.
This association is further decomposed into a direct own-region effect of $+0.0090$ and an indirect spillover to neighbouring regions of $+0.0050$.
This picture changes substantially as coherence departs from its mean. 
At one standard deviation above mean coherence the total betweenness effect rises to $+0.0348$ (more than double the mean-coherence estimate).
At one standard deviation below mean coherence the total effect of average betweenness turns negative at $-0.0068$, with both the direct ($-0.0043$) and indirect ($-0.0025$) channels reversing sign as well.
This means that regions characterised with high aggregate coherence are associated with higher average potential when their portfolio's average betweenness increase by one SD, whereas for low coherence regions, higher average betweenness negatively influence their average potential.
This is to say, that regions already endowed with bridging (general purpose) technologies would benefit more from higher aggregate industrial alignment on their average potential. 
The sign flip occurs at approximately $-0.67$ SD: regions below this coherence threshold experience a negative portfolio-level betweenness effect, while regions above it experience a positive one.
The interaction coefficient ($+0.0208$ total) is $142\%$ of the betweenness main effect ($+0.0140$).
Coherence conditions the direction and magnitude of this association.
A portfolio with bridge like technologies without adequate industrial alignment is not sufficient for regions to increase their aggregate potential.
To embed and activate those bridging connections into portfolio-level feasibility advantage a baseline of aggregate industrial alignment is required.
This is further illustrated in @tbl-eg_avg_coh, where we can see the association clearly for two groups of regions: those above the coherence threshold: Lombardia (ITC4, IT), Hannover (DE92, DE), and Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur (FRL0, FR); and those below it: Moravskoslezsko (CZ08, CZ), Zeeland (NL34, NL), and Cantabria (ES13, ES).
In this table we can see that even with low average betweenness (third, forth, and seventh deciles), regions above the coherence threshold have higher average regional potential (ninth decile), whereas for the regions below the coherence threshold (having average coherence below the second decile) have lower average potential despite having higher average betweenness than the regions above the same threshold.
This reflects known geographic dichotomies in Europe specifically the core-periphery divide. 




